@@ -355,6 +355,30 @@ resource. The available transformation types:
 - **yield map** - a map that returns an iterator (so a single row may generate many rows -
   `resource.add_yield_map`).
 
+`add_map` applies a function to each data item as it is extracted. The function receives a single
+item and must return the transformed item, which is then passed to the next step in the resource
+pipeline. You can chain multiple `add_map` calls (and mix them with `add_filter` and
+`add_yield_map`) to build a sequence of transformations.
+
+```py
+import dlt
+
+@dlt.resource
+def users():
+    yield {"id": 1, "email": "jane@example.com"}
+    yield {"id": 2, "email": "john@example.com"}
+
+def anonymize_user(user_data):
+    user_data["email"] = "REDACTED"
+    return user_data
+
+# apply the transformation to every item yielded by the resource
+for user in users().add_map(anonymize_user):
+    print(user)
+# {'id': 1, 'email': 'REDACTED'}
+# {'id': 2, 'email': 'REDACTED'}
+```
+
